# Round 30 — Euler-product positivity beyond 3-4-1: all anchored to the pole at s = 1

| mechanism | best achievement | why no fixed strip |
|-----------|------------------|--------------------|
| optimal trig polynomials (Mossinghoff–Trudgian 2015) | σ ≥ 1 - 1/(5.573412 log t) | width O(1/log t); degree improves the constant only |
| Stechkin's trick (1970) | sharper constants | pole-dominated positivity |
| Landau–Ingham smoothing | classical regions, density | oscillatory terms unsigned at fixed σ < 1 |
| Turán power sums | regions, density | discrete averages allow avoidance |
| Rankin–Selberg (Eisenstein) | regions for automorphic L | anchored to the pole |
| Selberg mollifier positivity | density, positive proportion | averages permit sparse off-line zeros |
| Selberg sieve / Λ² | elementary PNT (o(x)) | parity; no power saving |
| Dirichlet L, Landau–Page | regions except one Siegel zero | positivity allows a real zero near 1 |

Structural lesson: every known positivity argument draws its strength from the pole at s = 1,
whose influence decays like 1/log t; no fixed strip, let alone σ = 1/2, is reachable this way.
